### What is the Median of a Number Stream problem?

Design a data structure that accepts numbers one at a time from a continuous stream and can return the median of all numbers seen so far at any point. The median of a sorted list is the middle element if the count is odd, or the average of the two middle elements if the count is even. Insertion and median query should both be efficient.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class MedianFinder {

    private PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap; // smaller half
    private PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap; // larger half

    public MedianFinder() {
        maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
        minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
    }

    public void addNum(int amount) {
        if (maxHeap.isEmpty() || amount <= maxHeap.peek()) {
            maxHeap.offer(amount);
        } else {
            minHeap.offer(amount);
        }
        rebalance();
    }

    private void rebalance() {
        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1) {
            minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());
        } else if (minHeap.size() > maxHeap.size()) {
            maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());
        }
    }

    public double findMedian() {
        if (maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size()) {
            return (maxHeap.peek() + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;
        }
        return maxHeap.peek();
    }
}
```

### Why use two heaps instead of sorting?

Re-sorting the entire stream on every query costs O(n log n) per query, which is impractical for a live stream. With two heaps, each insertion costs O(log n) for the heap operation and each median query costs O(1) by peeking at the tops. For a stream of one million numbers, the two-heap approach does about 20 million operations total, while re-sorting would do about 20 trillion.

```java
// Comparison:
// Re-sort per query: O(n log n) per query, O(n^2 log n) total for n queries
// Two heaps:         O(log n) per insert, O(1) per query, O(n log n) total
// For n=1,000,000:   two heaps is about 1,000,000x faster
```

### How does insertion decide which heap receives the number?

If the max-heap is empty or the new number is at most the max-heap's root, the number belongs in the smaller half, so push it to the max-heap. Otherwise, it belongs in the larger half, so push it to the min-heap. This ensures that every element in the max-heap is less than or equal to every element in the min-heap.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class InsertionDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        // Stream: 500, 200, 800, 300
        // Step 1: 500 -> maxHeap empty, push to maxHeap
        //   maxHeap=[500], minHeap=[]
        // Step 2: 200 <= 500, push to maxHeap
        //   maxHeap=[500,200], minHeap=[], rebalance -> move 500
        //   maxHeap=[200], minHeap=[500]
        // Step 3: 800 > 200, push to minHeap
        //   maxHeap=[200], minHeap=[500,800]
        //   rebalance -> move 500 to maxHeap
        //   maxHeap=[500,200], minHeap=[800]
        // Step 4: 300 <= 500, push to maxHeap
        //   maxHeap=[500,300,200], minHeap=[800]
        //   rebalance -> move 500
        //   maxHeap=[300,200], minHeap=[500,800]
        //   Median = (300+500)/2 = 400
    }
}
```

### How does rebalancing maintain the size invariant?

After each insertion, check the sizes. If the max-heap has grown more than one larger than the min-heap, move its root to the min-heap. If the min-heap has grown larger than the max-heap, move its root to the max-heap. This convention keeps the max-heap either equal in size to the min-heap or exactly one larger, so the median is always accessible from the tops.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class RebalanceDemo {

    public static void showRebalance() {
        // Before: maxHeap=[500, 200], minHeap=[800]
        // maxHeap.size()=2 > minHeap.size()+1=2? No
        // minHeap.size()=1 > maxHeap.size()=2? No
        // No rebalance needed

        // Before: maxHeap=[500, 300, 200], minHeap=[800]
        // maxHeap.size()=3 > minHeap.size()+1=2? Yes
        // Move maxHeap root (500) to minHeap
        // After: maxHeap=[300, 200], minHeap=[500, 800]
    }
}
```

### How do you read the median?

If both heaps are the same size, the stream has an even number of elements, so the median is the average of the max-heap root (largest of the smaller half) and the min-heap root (smallest of the larger half). If the max-heap is larger, it holds the extra middle element, so its root is the median.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class MedianReadDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MedianFinder mf = new MedianFinder();
        mf.addNum(500);
        System.out.println("Median after 500: " + mf.findMedian());
        mf.addNum(200);
        System.out.println("Median after 200: " + mf.findMedian());
        mf.addNum(800);
        System.out.println("Median after 800: " + mf.findMedian());
        mf.addNum(300);
        System.out.println("Median after 300: " + mf.findMedian());
    }
}
```

```text
Median after 500: 500.0
Median after 200: 350.0
Median after 800: 500.0
Median after 300: 400.0
```

### What about using BigDecimal for banking amounts?

In a real banking system, amounts are BigDecimal to avoid floating-point precision loss. The max-heap needs a reverse-order comparator for BigDecimal, and the even-count median must be computed with BigDecimal division at a controlled scale and rounding mode.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class BigDecimalMedianFinder {

    private PriorityQueue<BigDecimal> maxHeap =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
    private PriorityQueue<BigDecimal> minHeap =
        new PriorityQueue<>();

    public void addAmount(BigDecimal amount) {
        if (maxHeap.isEmpty() || amount.compareTo(maxHeap.peek()) <= 0) {
            maxHeap.offer(amount);
        } else {
            minHeap.offer(amount);
        }
        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size() + 1) minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());
        else if (minHeap.size() > maxHeap.size()) maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());
    }

    public BigDecimal findMedian() {
        if (maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size()) {
            return maxHeap.peek().add(minHeap.peek())
                .divide(BigDecimal.valueOf(2), 2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
        }
        return maxHeap.peek();
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Operation | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| addNum | O(log n) | O(1) per call |
| findMedian | O(1) | O(1) |
| Total for n inserts | O(n log n) | O(n) for both heaps |
| Re-sort alternative | O(n^2 log n) total | O(n) |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is choosing the wrong rebalance direction, which causes the max-heap to grow too large or the min-heap to become larger than the max-heap. Another mistake is using floating-point arithmetic for money instead of BigDecimal.

```java
package com.bank.risk;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: rebalance allows minHeap to be 2 larger
    public static void wrongRebalance() {
        // Only checking maxHeap > minHeap + 1
        // but NOT checking minHeap > maxHeap
        // Fix: add the second check
    }

    // WRONG: using double for money
    public static void wrongMoney() {
        double a = 0.1 + 0.2; // 0.30000000000000004, not 0.3!
        // Fix: use BigDecimal for all monetary calculations
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers may ask how to handle removal of elements from the stream, how to find the median in a sliding window of size k (which requires a more complex augmented structure), and whether the approach works for a distributed system where numbers arrive on different machines.

```java
// Follow-up: sliding window median
// - Use two TreeMaps (multisets) instead of heaps
// - TreeMap supports O(log n) removal by value
// - Maintain the same size invariant
// - On each window slide: add new element, remove expired element, rebalance
```
